WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.922 --> 00:00:06.630
FILIP KNAPIK: Hi.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.720
I'm happy to present new updates
and exciting announcements

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.600
for serverless workflows
in Google Cloud.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.790
My name is Filip Knapik,
and I'm a product manager

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:15.998
working on workflow products.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.930
Workflows is a very broad term.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:22.960
Let's us first look
into some use cases

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.990
and needs around them.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.660
Let's imagine an
e-commerce application,

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:32.360
a workflow that needs to
orchestrate a couple of steps--

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:35.980
for instance, first, create an
invoice, then generate a PDF,

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.530
and then send it to a
customer over email.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:40.480
You want a workflow
product, not only

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.720
to manage and orchestrate
execution of those steps,

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.850
but also, for instance,
recover from errors

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:48.510
that those steps may encounter.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.450
Another use case that you may
want to manage with a workflow

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:55.830
product is processing of a
batch of transactions or events

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.500
or customer records, and
then for each of those,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.450
you may want to look
into the contents

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.250
and make a decision
on whether to take

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.950
a path with additional steps--
like, for instance, you're

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.750
looking into business
transactions,

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.760
checking if they're
overdue, and if so,

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.100
sending a reminder to
affected users or customers.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.820
But workflows don't
have to be focused

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.040
on business transactions only.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.920
They can also help in
automation of IT landscape.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.320
Like, for instance, you want
to start a virtual machine,

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.290
then wait for some time, check
whether specific conditions are

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.840
met-- like, for instance,
application is up and running--

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.140
and if so, take an action, and
if not take another action.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.330
And obviously, there
is many other use cases

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.820
in IT automation
space and others,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.680
and workflow
products are expected

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.680
to be very flexible
in automating work

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.160
across different types
of endpoints and APIs.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.850
Now, what do all those
use cases have in common?

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:50.400
There is a set of
foundational needs

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.580
that a product needs
to support in order

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:58.050
to enable implementation of
a workflow in a production

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.010
environment.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.380
The first and probably
most important

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.620
element is reliable
execution of steps.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:08.669
Again, in the business scenario,
in the production deployment,

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:12.630
we can't afford to fail
an execution of a workflow

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.880
just because there was a
temporary network outage,

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.930
and a single step was
affected, and a single step

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.730
wasn't properly executed.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.250
And as such, a
workflow product needs

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.680
to be able to recover
from some of those errors,

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.100
and handle exceptions
or retry steps as needed

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.310
to maximize the chance
of successful execution.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.310
Another feature that
a workflow product

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.600
needs to have is efficient
handover of the work.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.010
And it's not only handover
of the work and orchestration

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:41.970
of steps.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.370
What a workflow product
also needs to provide for

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:49.560
is transition and handover of
the data between those steps,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.870
because individual
steps can produce data

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:56.540
that other steps may rely
on in their execution.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.720
The decision execution
is another aspect

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.060
that is important for
workflow products.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.880
Imagine that you're building
a workflow that is processing

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.790
your orders, or orders
from your customers,

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:11.390
and you want to execute a
different path if an order

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:12.960
exceeds a specific threshold.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:16.340
Let's say beyond
or above $1,000,

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:18.920
you want to assign a
sales engineer or sales

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.410
specialist to that
customer to assist them,

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:22.950
because the deal is so big.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:25.640
And in order for you to
implement such decisions

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.380
as part of your
workflow directly,

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:30.950
you want to have a feature that
allows you to manage execution

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.650
in a different way, provided
that specific conditions

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:35.670
are met.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.150
Workflows don't always
integrate APIs or products

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:40.480
of the same type.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:44.880
You may want to orchestrate work
across different products using

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:48.180
different API languages,
using different HTTP

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.490
methods, different
authentication models, or even

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.073
host it in different places.

00:03:52.073 --> 00:03:53.490
And a workflow
product needs to be

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:56.580
able to integrate them all,
regardless of their diversity

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.790
and differences
in implementation.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.410
Last but not least
of the features

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:04.500
that I would like to call
out is a holistic perspective

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.200
on execution and management
of your workflows.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.710
First of all, imagine that a
third step of a workflow fails.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:12.240
You need to be
able to understand

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.760
what was the business
transaction that this step

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.279
failed in to be able
to recover from it,

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:20.730
or you may also need to see
how many workflow executions

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:24.690
you had for a specific customer,
or in a specific domain.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.090
All those features are very
important for a workflow

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:27.840
product.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:29.580
And when looking
into those, we have

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.580
realized that our
customers would greatly

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:36.300
benefit from an introduction
of a serverless workflow

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.680
orchestration product
as part of Google Cloud.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.170
And with that, I'm very happy
to announce a new product

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:46.330
in Google Cloud
family, Workflows.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:49.230
What this product is
expected to address

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:53.190
is orchestration and integration
of different types of APIs.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:57.360
First of all, integration of
serverless compute products.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.550
Let's say you want to
create a workflow consisting

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:01.200
of different cloud
functions, and you

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.430
want to effectively
and efficiently handle

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:04.990
the work over in between them.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:06.810
This is a great use
case for Workflows,

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:08.490
or the same for Cloud Run.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:10.200
But we don't want to stop here.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.410
You can also create a workflow
that, for instance, reads

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:17.250
the data from Firestore, or
pushes a message to Pub/Sub,

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:20.903
or doing any other operation
in other Google Cloud APIs.

00:05:20.903 --> 00:05:22.320
And this is also
not where we want

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:23.910
it to stop, because
we understand

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.400
that our customers are running
in hybrid environments,

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.940
and often rely on, for instance,
software as a service APIs,

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:32.730
hosted externally to our
cloud, and therefore,

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:35.880
Workflows products also
support direct calls

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.300
to external APIs as part
of the workflow definition,

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:43.350
including all of the handling of
errors, retries, and everything

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:44.800
around it.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.130
So this product is expected
to help our customers

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:50.250
in orchestration of work
across, practically speaking,

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:53.110
any of the APIs within
Google Cloud or external,

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:56.040
and integration of those
APIs in a holistic way

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:59.690
to enable implementation
of workflows.

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:02.150
The product is fully
managed and fully automated.

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:04.760
We want our customers to
focus on their business

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:07.910
logic of the workflow rather
than focusing on the underlying

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:08.780
infrastructure.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:12.440
Customers are not expected,
in case of Workflows,

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:15.912
to look into the storage
or think about patching

00:06:15.912 --> 00:06:17.120
of the underlying components.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.370
This is all taken care of.

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:21.970
Another element that is fully
taken care of is the scale.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:23.720
Regardless if you need
to run one workflow

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:26.990
execution per week, one per
day, or 1,000 per second,

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:27.950
we got it covered.

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:30.770
The product automatically
scales, and it also scales down

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:32.630
to 0, which means
that if you're not

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:34.400
running your workflows
for some time,

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.250
you don't have any
executions, your cost

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.480
is also going down to 0.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.760
We assume that some of our
customers may want to use

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:47.020
Workflows in
latency-sensitive workloads--

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.030
for instance, having
a customer, having

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:52.120
a user waiting on the other side
of the screen for an outcome

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:53.575
of a workflow execution.

00:06:53.575 --> 00:06:56.200
To support those cases, we have
engineered the product in a way

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.330
that step transitions
are very quick,

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:00.610
so that we don't introduce
unnecessary latency

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:02.650
in the execution of
a business workflow.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.720
We have also taken
care of time it

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:06.930
takes to start a new
workflow execution,

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:08.980
and there is no
so-called "cold start."

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:13.090
So what that practically means
is that starting a new workflow

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:15.610
execution is not
only quick, but also

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:18.750
predictable and deterministic.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:21.250
The product comes with
built-in security features--

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:24.160
first of all, you cannot make
unauthenticated requests,

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:25.950
so there is no
risk of somebody--

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:28.140
for instance, a bad
actor, or somebody

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:30.240
accidentally calling
out your workflow

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:32.937
and starting the execution
when not expected.

00:07:32.937 --> 00:07:34.770
The product also comes
with full integration

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:36.660
with identity and
access management

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:38.310
of Google Cloud,
which means that you

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:41.040
can use it to orchestrate the
work of other Google Cloud

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:42.840
products with built-in
authentication,

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.960
without worrying about tokens
and other elements related

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.940
to authentication to those.

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.880
If you need to make
requests to external APIs

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.820
and you need to manage secrets
or passwords accordingly,

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.780
you can use Secrets Manager
for this integration,

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:00.990
and make secure calls to
external APIs, as well.

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:03.750
And obviously, the product
comes with encryption at rest

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:09.082
and in transit, provided that
you are using HTTPS endpoints.

00:08:09.082 --> 00:08:10.540
By now, you are
probably wondering,

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:12.010
how do I model a workflow?

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:14.440
Can you show me an
example of how to do this?

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:15.772
And here it comes.

00:08:15.772 --> 00:08:17.230
On the left-hand
side, we're seeing

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:19.090
an example of the
workflow, which

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:21.640
consists of first a call
to a cloud function,

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:24.133
and then based on the outcome
of that called function,

00:08:24.133 --> 00:08:26.050
we're checking a condition
where a transaction

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:27.700
number is greater than 100.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:32.320
And if so, we want to trigger
an execution of a Cloud Run

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:33.340
deployment.

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:34.929
Otherwise, we do not.

00:08:34.929 --> 00:08:37.750
The right-hand side is
showing the source code

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:41.330
that implements this specific
workflow in our product.

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:44.169
So the first step is making
a call to a Cloud Function

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:47.710
as part of HTTP GET request, and
we're also passing some inputs

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:49.127
to that specific function.

00:08:49.127 --> 00:08:50.710
We're also saying
that we want to have

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:54.590
the outcome of that step start
in metric result variable.

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:57.220
This is a workflow variable
we're going to use later on.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:08:58.970
In the second step
of the workflow,

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:02.950
we have a conditional statement,
which checks whether a value--

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:04.360
in this case,
transaction number,

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:06.400
which is an outcome
of the first step--

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:07.570
is greater than 100.

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:11.080
And if so, we're making a call
to our Cloud Run deployment

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:12.943
using HTTPS POST method.

00:09:12.943 --> 00:09:14.860
And by the way, we're
also passing the outcome

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:18.070
of the first step to the
second step as part of the call

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:20.960
to this Cloud Run deployment.

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:23.110
So this is a short
example, but this is just

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:27.730
to show you how you can model
a workflow using our syntax,

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:28.590
using our language.

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:31.690
We took a lot of effort to
ensure that this language is

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:35.590
as brief, as clear to read
and write in as possible,

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:39.560
to give the maximum
benefit to our developers.

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:42.670
Let's have a quick look
at a demo of our product.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:44.200
Let's imagine that
we want to build

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:48.280
an e-commerce workflow that
sends an invoice to a customer.

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:51.430
The input to the workflow
will be an ID of a user

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:53.680
that we want to perform
this operation for.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.660
The first step of
the workflow we'll

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:58.810
be reading a record
for that specific user

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:01.260
from our Firestore database.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:03.930
If the amount due
equals to 0, we

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:05.890
are finishing the
execution at this point,

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.040
because there is nothing to do.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:10.020
Otherwise, we want to
generate an invoice

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:11.760
and send it over as an email.

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.340
We're going to use a Cloud
function to generate an invoice

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:17.130
as a PDF, and then
we want to make

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:20.610
a request to an external
service to send an email.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.200
Since this is an
external service,

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.390
we first need to
retrieve a secret

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:26.860
to be able to authenticate
to that service.

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:29.485
Let's see how this could be
implemented in our product.

00:10:29.485 --> 00:10:32.880
The source code of the
workflow is a YAML structure.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:34.650
In the first step
of the workflow,

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:37.470
we're making a request
to Firestore API,

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:41.000
passing an argument,
being an ID of a user,

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.423
and reading it
from the database.

00:10:43.423 --> 00:10:45.590
This obviously needs to be
an authenticated request,

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:47.840
as otherwise, Firestore
wouldn't let us read anything,

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:50.840
and this is what these two lines
of the workflow code to do.

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:53.030
Whatever the output
is of that step

00:10:53.030 --> 00:10:56.420
is stored in a user
record variable.

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:58.580
In the next step
of the workflow,

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:02.070
we're going to check whether a
specific field in the response,

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:06.860
amount due, equals to 0.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:11.890
Now, the response of the
first step was a JSON string,

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:13.720
and workflow engine
is smart enough

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:16.000
to convert that
into a dictionary

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.350
that we can use to validate
whether the amount matches

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:21.550
our expectations.

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:24.190
If this condition is met,
the workflow the execution

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:27.220
finishes, with no
amount due message.

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:29.650
Otherwise, it proceeds
to the next step,

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:33.490
being a function call
using a POST request,

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:36.160
to our PDF generator function.

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:38.140
Again, this is an
authenticated request,

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:39.580
because we don't
want our function

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:42.120
to be executed by anyone.

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:44.590
And as a request
body, we're also

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:48.130
passing the contents that
we want to have in our PDF

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:50.560
so that our function
knows what to generate.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:53.800
The output of that request
is stored in a PDF output

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:56.800
variable, which will actually
be the contents of the PDF file

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:58.998
that we want to use
as an attachment.

00:11:58.998 --> 00:12:01.540
In the next step, we're making
a request with Secrets Manager

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:07.570
API to read a specific secret,
in my case, called email key.

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:10.510
And then we're going to use
that secret in the request

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:13.300
to our email sending
service, and this

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:16.300
is what the last step
of the workflow does.

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:19.900
And as you can see, we're also
including here an attachment,

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:22.540
being the PDF output
body, which is

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:25.780
the response of the
function generating our PDF.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:27.850
And as the very last
step of the workflow,

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:30.790
we are responding
with a message,

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:34.270
saying that the user was sent an
invoice for a specific amount.

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:36.520
So with that, let's test
it out, and let's see

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:39.050
if it actually works.

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:41.720
Let me first show you the
entry for a user we're

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:45.890
going to be testing it
for, which is smith.j.

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:49.490
Apparently, he has
$784 amount that we

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:50.948
need to send an invoice for.

00:12:50.948 --> 00:12:52.490
And by the way, if
you're interested,

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:55.250
the function that we're
using to generate the PDF

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:56.780
is based on an
open-source package

00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:59.580
available as a PyPy module.

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:04.520
So let's now execute this
workflow and see if it works.

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:09.395
So as an input, I'm passing
an ID of our user, smith.j,

00:13:09.395 --> 00:13:13.570
and I'm requesting an
execution of the workflow.

00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:15.320
As you can see, execution
state is active,

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:17.970
but let's refresh
and see if it's done.

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:18.990
Apparently, it is.

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:21.540
So the output of the
workflow is the user

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:26.280
was sent an invoice for $784,
which is what we expect,

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:28.630
because this is what
we saw in the database.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:32.500
Now, I also happen to have an
email account for that user,

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:34.920
and as you can see, we
actually got an email

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:38.760
that we were expecting
with a PDF attachment.

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:39.990
Let's have a look.

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:42.300
And the value matches
our expectation,

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:45.430
because this is exactly
what we saw in the database.

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:47.940
So with this short demo,
you are able to see

00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:51.990
that we are able to orchestrate
the work of different Google

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:55.350
Cloud products, including
Firestore, Cloud

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:57.930
Function, or Google
Secrets Manager,

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:00.150
as well as making a request
to an external service

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:02.250
to send our email,
and orchestration

00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:04.650
of all of those tasks
was performed flawlessly

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:07.430
by Workflows.

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:09.380
Let's now have a look
into the pricing model.

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:12.590
First of all, we wanted to
design a pricing model that

00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:15.710
follows the promise of
serverless scalability, which

00:14:15.710 --> 00:14:19.940
means we want the cost of using
the product to grow exactly

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:21.440
as the usage grows.

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:23.600
In other words, the
moment you go down

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:25.790
to zero because you
have an idle period,

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:28.070
or you don't need to execute
a workflow for whatever

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:30.290
business reasons, we
don't want to charge

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:32.450
for this specific scenario.

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:34.490
And therefore, the more
you run your workflows,

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:36.920
the higher, obviously,
the cost will be,

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:39.140
following exactly your
demand and following

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:41.100
the growth of your business.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:44.900
We differentiate two types of
steps in our pricing model.

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:48.290
The pricing is based on
number of executed steps

00:14:48.290 --> 00:14:49.220
in a workflow.

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:52.520
Internal steps relate
to all the elements

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:54.440
you can call within
the Google Cloud,

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:56.990
like Cloud Functions
or Cloud Run

00:14:56.990 --> 00:14:59.030
or internal variable
assignments,

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:02.450
or expression evaluations
within the Google Cloud workflow

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:03.860
product itself.

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:05.660
For all those
operations, the cost

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:09.290
is $0.01 per 1,000
steps executed.

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:12.600
For external requests,
which are all the HTTP

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:14.570
your requests going
to the outside world

00:15:14.570 --> 00:15:18.425
to non-Google domain names,
the cost is 2 and 1/2 cents

00:15:18.425 --> 00:15:20.720
per 1,000 steps.

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:23.940
For both of those types of
steps, there is a free tier.

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:26.000
So if you just want
to give it a try,

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:28.120
you want to see where this
product is capable of,

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:31.230
or you are a hobbyist and
you just want to try it out,

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:36.020
you can use a free tier and
basically get experience

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:38.300
from using the product,
and then decide

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:41.370
whether you want to move on
with further implementations.

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:45.380
So the free tier is 5,000
internal steps per month,

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:49.250
or 2,000 external
steps per month.

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:52.100
With this, I wanted to
very warmly invite you

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:55.880
all to our Beta that
is available right

00:15:55.880 --> 00:15:58.340
now to our product
to start using it.

